</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/670b3f24-8994-11f1-8169-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C37Y7T3.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/670b3f24-8994-11f1-8169-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C37Y7T3.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">80085b8c-8990-11f1-9a84-d7bb134e8900</guid><pubDate>Mon, 27 Jul 2026 09:24:52 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/670b3f24-8994-11f1-8169-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C37Y7T3.jpg">AFP - -</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>International</category><title>France and Spain race to contain wildfires before new heatwave</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/international/20260727-france-and-spain-battle-vast-wildfires-as-fresh-heatwave-approaches</link><description>
Firefighters in France and Spain rushed to contain the worst wildfires in living memory on Tuesday, seizing crucial hours before the arrival of another wave of searing temperatures.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/174c585c-89de-11f1-b830-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-26T162808Z-1025304720-RC2OLMARJ559-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-FRANCE-WILDFIRE.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/174c585c-89de-11f1-b830-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-26T162808Z-1025304720-RC2OLMARJ559-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-FRANCE-WILDFIRE.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">08ea9bda-8983-11f1-b9cc-e5a49bdb74b3</guid><pubDate>Mon, 27 Jul 2026 06:36:51 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/174c585c-89de-11f1-b830-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-26T162808Z-1025304720-RC2OLMARJ559-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-FRANCE-WILDFIRE.jpg">REUTERS - Manon Cruz</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>International</category><title>No snakes in church and don't eat the frogs: the weird world of US animal laws</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/international/20260726-no-snakes-in-church-and-don-t-eat-the-frogs-the-weird-world-of-us-animal-laws</link><description>
A few keywords in a search engine will bring up any number of strange laws involving animals in the United States. Many are urban myths: there is no law specifically prohibiting driving a car in Massachusetts with a gorilla in the back seat, although no one would dispute that would be a questionable idea. Others, however, are very real. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/74e73070-74dc-11f1-a795-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Handling-serpents-at-the-Pentecostal-Church-of-God-Kentucky-by-Russell-Lee-NARA-541335.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/74e73070-74dc-11f1-a795-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Handling-serpents-at-the-Pentecostal-Church-of-God-Kentucky-by-Russell-Lee-NARA-541335.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4e4e53e2-850a-11f1-84e4-4db1a4532f94</guid><pubDate>Sun, 26 Jul 2026 19:21:52 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/74e73070-74dc-11f1-a795-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Handling-serpents-at-the-Pentecostal-Church-of-God-Kentucky-by-Russell-Lee-NARA-541335.jpg">© Russell Lee, National Archives and Records Administration NAID 541335, via Wikicommons</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>International</category><title>Trauma and taboos: how Portugal takes care of migrants' mental health</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/international/20260726-trauma-and-taboos-how-portugal-takes-care-of-migrants-mental-health</link><description>
Migration is increasingly essential to Portugal's economy, but the mental health of many new arrivals to the country is affected by the trauma of what they have left behind, long and arduous journeys, and the racism that greets them, fuelled by far-right rhetoric. In addition, mental health treatment is often impeded by cultural and linguistic barriers. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0ac2a64a-6be8-11f1-bc30-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/What-Health-Portugal-sant%C3%A9-mentale.jpeg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0ac2a64a-6be8-11f1-bc30-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/What-Health-Portugal-sant%C3%A9-mentale.jpeg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a991009e-85c1-11f1-a361-7373ca996252</guid><pubDate>Sun, 26 Jul 2026 12:59:38 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0ac2a64a-6be8-11f1-bc30-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/What-Health-Portugal-sant%C3%A9-mentale.jpeg">© Sofia Peyssonneau / Fondação Aga Khan</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>International</category><title>Main suspect in Berlin Pride attack shot dead by German police forces</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/international/20260725-several-people-are-injured-at-berlin-s-lgbtq-march-after-a-car-drives-into-the-crowd</link><description>
The suspect in the deadly Berlin Pride attack that left one person dead and 29 injured was shot and killed by police during a confrontation on Sunday in a suburb of the city, authorities said. 
